    "q-125501": {
      "dom_id": "step-q125501",
      "name": "Question 7",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14221961/assets/ee674d6140d9326894d607ca9a3feede580ce68bd56ca762a013a9602a657832.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y)$ shown above, and let $C$ be the path where the curve is traversed from $A$ to $B.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0$\n\nII. ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0$\n\nIII. ${\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$",
      "worked_solution": "Remember that ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ gives the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along $C:$\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion  of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. Since the force $F$ acts perpendicular to the motion of the particle as it moves from $A$ to $B,$ it neither helps nor hinders its motion. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14221961/assets/d69e4c3dee5a346baae0accffc4d234b5b84e0402d107b5d1a24160a796992dd.png)\n\n \nSo, ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. If we reverse the orientation of $C,$ then the situation with regards to the work done by $F$ is similar to the previous case.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14221961/assets/61a1ebee96c7082485b318d5bcc1f55a42c0f4589b0aad9edf09e4deb82a2403.png)\n\n \nSo, ${\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
