    "q-125502": {
      "dom_id": "step-q125502",
      "name": "Question 10",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14221961/assets/8aed400e7768b70f4532642418d83b36d9e32aa84db87c71ea6d902504930bc4.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y)$ shown above, and let $C$ be the path where the semiellipse is traversed from $A$ to $B.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$\n\nII. ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0$\n\nIII. ${\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0$",
      "worked_solution": "Remember that ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ gives the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along $C:$\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion  of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. Since the force $F$ acts perpendicular to the motion of the particle as it moves from $A$ to $B,$ it neither helps nor hinders its motion. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14221961/assets/d8e05de34bf9ddfdbf9580b1df32eb9e091f56f76ac3616cbb1cd81d5d212a86.png)\n\n \nSo, ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. If we reverse the orientation of $C,$ then the situation with regards to the work done by $F$ is similar to the previous case.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14221961/assets/17423916013677c1476df5a4368ab9eec81671b04b4ca29a0e702b20ddc818f2.png)\n\n \nSo, ${\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
